Hexagon HTP: the chunked gated delta rule on the HMX for a batch of tokens

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9, with patches 0001 thru 0004 applied.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gdn-chunk-ops.c         new: the chunked kernel
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gated-delta-net-ops.c   routes a batch of tokens to the chunked kernel
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-ctx.h               the prototype
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/CMakeLists.txt          the new source
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp            the switch GGML_HEXAGON_GDN_CHUNK
    tests/test-backend-ops.cpp                        GATED_DELTA_NET cases over batches

How to apply, from the llama.cpp root:

    git apply patches/hexagon-fusion/0005-hexagon-htp-gdn-chunked-hmx.patch
    cmake --build build-snapdragon -j

Push to the phone: libggml-htp-v79.so (ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/), libggml-hexagon.so (bin/) and
test-backend-ops (bin/).

This patch does not depend on the op fusion of patches 0002 thru 0004, and the switch
GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION_STATE does not reach it. The kernel is inside the plain GATED_DELTA_NET op,
which the backend runs for every batch of tokens. Its own switch is GGML_HEXAGON_GDN_CHUNK.

Problem: GATED_DELTA_NET at 512 tokens takes 6.5 ms per op, 0.12 TFLOPS

The kernel of the backend runs the recurrence one token after the other. Per token and head it
scales the state, takes a dot product with k over each of the 128 state rows, adds a rank one
update, and takes a dot product with q. That is 11 HVX operations per state vector for 3 useful
multiply and add operations, plus one reduction per row. The op runs at the HVX rate, and the HMX
stays idle.

The new kernel takes chunks of 64 tokens. Inside a chunk the recurrence becomes matrix products,
and those run on the HMX in fp16 with f32 accumulation. The state stays in f32 in VTCM.

The algorithm, for one (sequence, head) row and one chunk of C tokens, with the state S [D x D]
(S[i][j], i the key index, j the value index), the log decay g_t, and gamma_t the sum of g_s for
s <= t:

    E[t][s]  = exp(gamma_t - gamma_s) for s <= t, 0 for s > t         HVX, f32
    A        = (K beta) K^T                                            HMX
    T        = (I + E .* A strictly below the diagonal)^-1             HVX, f32, forward substitution
    P        = Q K^T                                                   HMX
    W        = T (K beta e^gamma),  U = T (V beta)                     HMX
    WS       = W S                                                     HMX
    Delta    = U - WS                                                  HVX, fp16
    O        = (scale E .* P) Delta + (scale Q e^gamma) S              HMX, two products, one accumulator
    S_next   = e^gamma_C S + (K e^(gamma_C - gamma))^T Delta           HMX product, f32 update

Each chunk is three HMX jobs with HVX work between them. A worker thread owns rows (sequence, head)
and holds its own VTCM buffers, 440 KB at head size 128. The DMA of the next chunk of q, k and v
starts before the first job of the chunk in work. The three jobs of the threads go to the one HMX
queue thread, with a spinlock for the pushes, and each worker waits on the done flag of its own
descriptor. The op flushes the queue at the end.

Why the result equals the reference, and by how much

The chunked form is an exact rewrite of the sequential recurrence of
ggml_compute_forward_gated_delta_net, not an approximation. Inside a chunk the state after token t
is S_t = D_t S_0 + (the rank one updates of the tokens up to t), where D_t = e^gamma_t is a scalar.
Substitution of that form into the delta rule gives a linear system for the C update vectors whose
matrix is unit lower triangular, (I + E .* A strictly below the diagonal). T is its inverse, thus W
and U carry the exact updates, and O and S_next are the exact output and state. The host program
scratchpad/gdn_chunk_check.cpp runs this dataflow next to the sequential reference: in f32 the NMSE
is 1e-13, which is the rounding of the two orders of summation.

The kernel is not bit exact against the CPU, and no HMX kernel of this backend is. The error is the
fp16 rounding at each HMX input and output. The same host program, with fp16 rounding at every HMX
input and output, gives these NMSE values against the f32 reference:

    tokens  head size  gate range        NMSE output   NMSE state
    512     128        [-0.5,  -1e-4]    3.5e-07       1.6e-07
    672     128        [-0.5,  -1e-4]    3.5e-07       1.9e-07
    672     128        [-20,   -1e-4]    2.9e-07       1.6e-07
    512     128        [-0.01, -1e-4]    4.3e-07       3.1e-07
    100     128        [-0.5,  -1e-4]    3.6e-07       1.6e-07
    70      64         [-0.5,  -1e-4]    3.0e-07       2.6e-07
    65      96         [-0.5,  -1e-4]    2.8e-07       1.9e-07
    33      32         [-0.5,  -1e-4]    2.7e-07       1.8e-07

The value 3e-07 is the square of the fp16 resolution, 2^-11. The error does not grow with the token
count, because each chunk multiplies the state that carries the old error by e^gamma_C, which is at
most 1. A gate near zero, which keeps the state alive over all ten chunks of 672 tokens, gives the
same 3e-07 as a gate that decays in one token. The tests below use a tolerance of 1e-5, thus a
margin of 20 on the measurement.

The one-token path keeps the f32 kernel, thus decode does not change at all. A batch under 32
tokens also keeps it, because the fixed work of a chunk does not pay off there.

The tests

test-backend-ops runs GATED_DELTA_NET over batches against the CPU reference. The first three cases
are the shapes of Qwen3.5-2B: 16 heads of 128 with 512 tokens is one text prefill batch, and 672
tokens is one image. The counts 672, 100, 70, 65, 33 and 66 are not multiples of the chunk of 64,
thus the tail of the last chunk runs in each of them. The other cases cover the head sizes 32, 64
and 96, two sequences, a k head that two v heads share, a gate that decays in one token, and
permuted inputs.

The gate of the cases that were already there runs from -20, which decays the state in one or two
tokens and hides every error that travels between chunks. The new cases keep the gate near zero.

The switch

GGML_HEXAGON_GDN_CHUNK=0 keeps the sequential kernel for every batch. The host gives the value to
the DSP in the kernel params of each GATED_DELTA_NET op, thus one build runs both sides of an A/B
test. The default is 1.

The kernel also returns to the sequential one, per op, when: the gate is a vector (KDA, which
Qwen3.5 does not use), K is not 1, the batch has fewer than 32 tokens, the head size is not a
multiple of 32 or is over 128, a row of q, k or v is not contiguous, the gates of the heads of one
token are not contiguous, the HMX is off, or the VTCM does not hold the buffers of the threads. The
profile shows which kernel ran: 6.5 ms per op is the sequential one.

Expected speed

The HMX work is 1.5 GFLOP per layer of the 2B at 512 tokens, about 0.25 ms at the rate that the
matmuls of this backend reach. The HVX work is about 0.3 ms per layer over six threads, and the DMA
of q, k and v is 12 MB per layer, which is the same traffic that the sequential kernel reads. The
expectation is 0.5 to 1 ms per layer against 6.5 ms, thus about 25 ms of the 235 ms that the 36 ops
of one pp512 batch take today.
